#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124248 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "d135b6c2-8257-5ba5-ac15-3d43a9abd3d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5444" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3 \\to \\mathbb{R}^3$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T$ is invertible? \n\n- $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$\n\n- $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=0$\n\n- $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = 3$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ into $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071474/assets/407378634431fde7e66d07a36239276173d6ae121f22ef4567cb1f2b28688624.png) \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124353 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "2086adfe-9a09-5b60-8f5e-9edb0c700b98" :question/math-academy-id "e-9985" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3\\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $[1, 2, 1]^T \\not\\in  \\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}),$ then $\\textrm{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\mathbb{R}^3$\n\n- $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T})\\ne\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ into $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071474/assets/360d6855ad322ec1ffbfdae960754f04522a33ded3e3a7e15711f0099b312ef2.png) \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $[1, 2, 1]^T \\not\\in  \\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}),$ we must have that $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the vector $[1, 2, 1]^T$ does not lie in $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$, then $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3.$ Therefore, according to the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3,$ the transformation $\\mathbf T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124354 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eead7563-6231-5490-9a10-721d09133352" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "427d3dc9-9ae8-5c40-b75c-519db84f2bc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-9986" :question/problem "$\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071474/assets/e6be87200a2cbb3709d3f398fefad0c547071edb703f88d8859d6ee0c74cc5dd.png) \n$\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ What should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071474/assets/32e958f4890d733ebc1856a8ca317ff6b52ed797f04a01369d6356c4130240c8.png) \n$\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10229a12-3c81-5b0d-b244-54226c4324dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371281} {:answer/id #uuid "fca0f671-cf70-53f7-bb7a-4c096773a1f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371282} {:answer/id #uuid "3e5507dc-84a1-538a-a484-88e464cda3ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371283} {:answer/id #uuid "acd12752-26d5-5969-b0af-997b8bafce84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371284} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8e3673-9bd7-577a-a98e-d55d261addf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fca0f671-cf70-53f7-bb7a-4c096773a1f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371282} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2aaf7d8-06ad-51fe-aebd-030946a65770" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e5c15541-b8e5-5d30-a6d3-ead1f390de3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-140079" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{7}\\to {R}^{7}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$ for some distinct vectors  ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}?$ \n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- $T$ is invertible\n- $\\text{rank}(T)=7$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{: