k}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $\\text{rank}(T)=5,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $T$ is invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371293 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eead7563-6231-5490-9a10-721d09133352" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c5d1cba-7222-55ba-9e5a-09b6ea994409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371295} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1bfd94-b9d0-57fd-9edb-436327562c86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R\\times R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371296} {:answer/id #uuid "d955becb-d80a-5fc8-a0cf-f6bbc9646c5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{n}\\times {R}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371297} {:answer/id #uuid "3f78dabe-1938-54ca-9f15-5cf65a0f4978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371298} {:answer/id #uuid "b43d4ca1-061b-5ef8-960b-d636247e2273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b43d4ca1-061b-5ef8-960b-d636247e2273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371299} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9224eb4-eac6-5392-b1ef-5f2024b5a5e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371294}] :question/difficulty {:db/