izing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a89e4496-6911-5903-b541-99fd57a9776f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{Null}(T)$ and $\\text{Ker}(T)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371309} {:answer/id #uuid "f4a0eae5-a171-549c-a726-a9a777708ea7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{Im}(T)$ and $\\text{Ker}(T)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371310} {:answer/id #uuid "03502f79-1d4b-5ad4-b08d-1e85fecb327e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{Im}(T)$ and $\\text{det}(T)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371311} {:answer/id #uuid "d16b5107-ad7f-5058-97b8-85a017d106d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{Ker}(T)$ and $\\text{Domain}(T)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371312} {:answer/id #uuid "667525ec-a247-5b8d-ad2a-3e434de44645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{Domain}(T)$ and $\\text{Range}(T)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4a0eae5-a171-549c-a726-a9a777708ea7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{Im}(T)$ and $\\text{Ker}(T)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371310} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd8ceb31-7b4c-5108-936e-3a992963d3cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dfbf91fd-f702-5f3b-a07f-c82e7e72afa4" :question/math-academy-id "q-141454" :question/problem "$T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & ={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & ={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ **From top to bottom**, which of the following should be placed into the blank spaces to make the statements true?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom the missing expression are $\\text{Im}(T)$ and $\\text{Ker}(T).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eead7563-6231-5490-9a10-721d09133352" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Parts of the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3452a7f-3f0d-5150-a3db-3286003d8b32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ and $n$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371316} {:answer/id #uuid "da04033f-440f-5c93-9aca-6d27a76aa5a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}^{n}$ and $n$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371317} {:answer/id #uuid "71708ca6-ed0c-5dc7-a5dd-9de3be78b023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R∖{0}$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371318} {:answer/id #uuid "b76b3717-3082-572b-9561-182aa89b801f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{Domain}(T)$ and $n-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371319} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f15320-3726-51ea-b0bb-a319c54da417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}^{n}$ and $\\text{det}(T)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da04033f-440f-5c93-9aca-6d27a76aa5a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${R}^{n}$ and $n$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371317} :answer-field/id #uuid "98d75efb-8ddb-5665-9de5-aa60c49d0177" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5235cf05-30f6-5dfe-ac0c-7d65e7115de0" :question/math-academy-id "q-141457" :question/problem "$T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)= \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)= \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ **From top to bottom**, which of the following should be placed into the blank spaces to make the statements true?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the missing expressions are ${R}^{n}$ and $n.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07377e4f-6501-59e8-a4ea-b097fb45031a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371323} {:answer/id #uuid "f33032ef-3d2d-531f-a37e-a9081928e853" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371324} {:answer/id #uuid "2c8440cd-e694-508d-b694-7db873e958fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371325} {:answer/id #uuid "15308645-3e58-5493-aa09-8622b720ea4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371326} {:answer/id #uuid "ae06088a-a465-52c4-949b-be84f63f0258" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f33032ef-3d2d-531f-a37e-a9081928e853" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371324} :answer-field/id #uuid "f25cb538-21d5-54a4-9e90-f2162a989ad4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e6231d52-ba27-5624-97d0-175d96fdf993" :question/math-academy-id "q-141460" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{7}\\to {R}^{7}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T(x)=v$ has no solution for some vector $v?$ \n\n- $T$ is not invertible\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T(x)=v$ has no solution, we have that $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ hence $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7}.$ According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371328 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caeb2f46-e599-554b-8c3e-d0c29cbcbc2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371330} {:answer/id #uuid "64c0bc92-7099-51f5-8533-1ec0b72af270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371331} {:answer/id #uuid "8320e71c-e27b-597d-affe-a9596b176551" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371332} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9a48c0-67a0-5d20-8d27-7fb996eb2b4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371333} {:answer/id #uuid "e2661115-9e68-5340-9609-eb68917633d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd9a48c0-67a0-5d20-8d27-7fb996eb2b4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371333} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2e60ee9-826d-568e-bc51-3dbe90016fd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff9f8824-5cc2-5f05-b6a5-d27eacc85092" :question/math-academy-id "q-141461" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{4}\\to {R}^{4}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T$ is a bijection? \n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)=4$\n- $T(v)=0$ for some $v\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{4}$ into ${R}^{4},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=4 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)=4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0},$ which means there is no non-zero vector $v$ such that $T(v)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c8ac925-9461-590a-97c7-65ff7a26cfde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371337} {:answer/id #uuid "49efbb4c-1655-5e26-a2ce-f2e988f00a9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371338} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa6c946-ca92-51e5-9b62-fc6e1fb1f794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371339} {:answer/id #uuid "b3fb2d58-8c68-567a-b31a-d9f5631dc9f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371340} {:answer/id #uuid "148e53fd-a596-553b-ac3e-f4ab467d3a30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49efbb4c-1655-5e26-a2ce-f2e988f00a9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371338} :answer-field/id #uuid "39736f7e-2d2b-5b1a-9767-25f624c6cef1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "daea3b8e-c165-5730-8762-171e1c8211d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-141495" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\in {R}^{3}.$  Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $2T({v}_{1})-3T({v}_{2})=0$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne k{v}_{2}$ for all $k\\in R$ then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3}$\n- $T$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $2T({v}_{1})-3T({v}_{2})=0$ and $T$ is a linear transformation, we have $T(2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2})=0$ which means $2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$   Since ${v}_{1}\\ne k{v}_{2}$,  we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the transformation $T$ is not a bijection. Since $T$ is not a bijection, then by the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3},$ the corresponding matrix $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371342 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f92f98e6-531d-57e4-9d5b-7e47eed6a947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371344} {:answer/id #uuid "b7a13a42-6969-570f-86a3-f47c986ae4b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371345} {:answer/id #uuid "48f9b536-be70-57d1-befd-cc0d82c930d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371346} {:answer/id #uuid "a1788616-224c-52f3-a1f8-6b7216b5c3ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371347} {:answer/id #uuid "6afb3470-2bba-53e7-b44b-c91c9a0f0265" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f92f98e6-531d-57e4-9d5b-7e47eed6a947" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371344} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9f6182c-af37-5818-ad99-4159e95df563" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "afdc05f9-0600-581b-8a60-cfa4085c48ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-141569" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{4}\\to {R}^{4}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If $\\text{nullity}(T)=4,$ then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4}$\n- $T$ is not invertible\n- $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 4$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{4}$ into ${R}^{4},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=4 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0,$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that $T$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a09a96e5-7ad2-5229-9767-1c7ce4a117fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371351} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1b7eba-7509-5d3c-b16d-75b16fe8c25b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371352} {:answer/id #uuid "f012c9e1-e498-5dd6-b200-0102cd1cb265" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371353} {:answer/id #uuid "5365c474-f9b5-5b41-a943-6dcee7d2d89a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371354} {:answer/id #uuid "c79cbdce-e6e0-5869-b258-ca0a3ebe56ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a1b7eba-7509-5d3c-b16d-75b16fe8c25b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371352} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8296f36-13a2-5582-92e9-d785ef0d4eb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f204c4fc-39db-5932-8093-ae24fbf567d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-141584" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{6}\\to {R}^{6}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2}$ vectors such that $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})$, then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$\n- $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6}$\n- $T$ is not a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{6}$ into ${R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=6 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$\n\nSince $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\nT({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${v}_{1}\\ne {v}_{2},$ we must have that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible. Since $T$ is not invertible, by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{6}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem implies that the transformation $T$ is not a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371356 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdf0a51c-8aa3-5653-94ca-60dac5cbfe0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371358} {:answer/id #uuid "05bb27d9-d8a0-53a8-b673-32bd391e111b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371359} {:answer/id #uuid "fd910347-8630-5620-b63a-a1e8821fc530" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371360} {:answer/id #uuid "c4edcd50-ba48-521c-aa6e-5f4316600b1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371361} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd660bb-9af1-5cff-8b14-5cdce8c0876f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdf0a51c-8aa3-5653-94ca-60dac5cbfe0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371358} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c5c6347-8bf2-523f-bedb-da3e2aaff926" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "281adabe-ae00-5e13-8596-d94b7cec2a6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-38905" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{6}\\to {R}^{6}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T$ is a bijection? \n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6}$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)=6$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{6}$ into ${R}^{6},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=6 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{6}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is a bijection, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)=6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "670e6b94-78e6-5608-a71c-3d8712b43c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371365} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc0b188-0b4e-52ce-b58c-9010c7299569" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371366} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8fd168-bff1-5fb9-810e-e656cf134e67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371367} {:answer/id #uuid "3f198485-39f2-5186-81aa-ce4da0be8005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371368} {:answer/id #uuid "5d57eb01-cfbe-53a2-a6cc-9c4dc2167fe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd8fd168-bff1-5fb9-810e-e656cf134e67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371367} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebcd7743-69c0-58e2-98b2-427225649d13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a256295e-1638-51d8-b1c8-ce671e7522e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-38907" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)?$\n\n- $\\text{rank}(T)=3$\n- $T$ is invertible\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)>0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)$ we must have that $\\text{rank}(T)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5,$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$ In turn, this means that $\\text{nullity}(T)>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5df8913-3a20-56ef-89a7-01dbbea7c04f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123383} {:answer/id #uuid "c00cf7cc-2217-517c-bcaf-c7bad9f5fdf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #e